description.\n\nModel family: The cert covers a family of instruments with many meter sizes (DN50 through DN600), two accuracy classes, plus reduced bore variants. So model_family should include models. That's a lot of models: class 1 has 14 sizes; class 2 has 14 full bore + 4 reduced bore = 18. Should I model each as model_id? The family covers DN50...DN600. Perhaps simpler: model_id like \"DN50\" etc. But accuracy class also differs. Hmm.\n\nThe cleanest approach: matrix_tables for Table 2 (class 1), Table 3 (class 2), Table 4 (indicating device), Table 5 (software versions). And model_level characteristics for flow rates per size and class.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll define model_family with models keyed by size and class and bore. E.g. model_id \"DN50 class 1\", ... That gets heavy. Alternatively, keep model_family.models as the meter sizes: DN50, DN65, ..., DN600 (14 sizes), with attributes like bore_variant: full/reduced. And model_level characteristics with per-class values.\n\nActually, I think the best representation:\n- matrix_tables: \n  1. \"General characteristics of the family of instruments with accuracy class 1\" with columns meter_size, diameter_in_outlet_mm, q1_min, q2, q3_permanent, q4_overload, ratio_q3_q1.\n  2. Same for class 2 (includes reduced bore rows).\n  3. \"Indicating device characteristics\" — meter size groups, indicating range, verification scale interval.\n  4. \"Approved software versions\" — software_version, crc_checksum, remarks.\n- model_level characteristics: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 per model, where model identified by size + class. Since class 1 and class 2 differ in some values (e.g., DN150 class1 Q1=3.9375 vs class2 Q1=2.52; DN200 class1 Q3=1000 vs class2 1600; etc.), the model dimension must include class.\n\nLet me check differences between Table 2 (class 1) and Table 3 (class 2):\n\nClass 1:\n- DN50: Ø50, Q1 0.315, Q2 0.504, Q3 63, Q4 78.75, ratio 200\n- DN65: 65, 0.5, 0.8, 100, 125, 200\n- DN80: 80, 0.8, 1.28, 160, 200, 200\n- DN100: 100, 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN125: 125, 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250\n- DN150: 150, 3.9375, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160\n- DN200: 200, 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n- DN250: 250, 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN300: 300, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN350: 350, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\n- DN400: 400, 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\n- DN450: 450, 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n- DN500: 500, 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n- DN600: 600, 50.400, 80.640, 6300, 7875, 125\n\nClass 2:\n- DN50: 0.315, 0.504, 63, 78.75, 200\n- DN50 RB: 0.158, 0.25, 63, 78.75, 400\n- DN65: 0.5, 0.8, 100, 125, 200\n- DN65 RB: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n- DN80: 0.8, 1.28, 160, 200, 200\n- DN80 RB: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN100: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n- DN100 RB: 0.63, 1.00, 250, 312.5, 400\n- DN125: 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250\n- DN150: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n- DN200: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN250: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n- DN300: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN350: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n- DN400: 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n- DN450: 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN500: 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n- DN600: 40, 64, 10000, 12500, 250\n\nSo many differences between classes. The model dimension should be size × class × (bore). That's 14 + 18 = 32 model variants. That's a lot but doable.\n\nAlternatively, I can keep models as just sizes and use config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\" for values. Hmm. The schema's config_level: attribute × config-axis matrix, values: {condition, value}. This could work: e.g. attribute \"q3_permanent_flow\", axis \"accuracy_class and meter size\"... but config_level values only have one condition field, so combining two axes (size and class) in one condition string is awkward but possible: condition \"class 1, DN150\".\n\nI think the cleanest is model_level where model_id = \"DN150 class 1\" etc. But model_family.models listing 32 entries is verbose yet correct.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Perhaps keep it simpler: model_family models = the 14 sizes (DN50..DN600) since that's the \"family of instruments\" concept, with attributes {accuracy_classes: [1,2]}. Then model_level characteristics use model ids like \"DN150/class 1\". Hmm, but then values reference models not in the model list.\n\nI think being explicit is better. Let me enumerate models with model_id like \"DN50 class 1 full bore\" ... too wordy. Maybe \"DN50/C1\", \"DN50/C2\", \"DN50/C2/RB\".\n\nLet me define:\n- model_id: \"DN50 class 1\", \"DN65 class 1\", ..., \"DN600 class 1\" (14)\n- \"DN50 class 2\", \"DN50 class 2 reduced bore\", \"DN65 class 2\", \"DN65 class 2 reduced bore\", \"DN80 class 2\", \"DN80 class 2 reduced bore\", \"DN100 class 2\", \"DN100 class 2 reduced bore\", \"DN125 class 2\", \"DN150 class 2\", ..., \"DN600 class 2\" (18)\n\nTotal 32 models. Then model_level entries:\n- diameter_in_outlet: per model (mm)\n- minimum_flow_q1 (m³/h)\n- transitional_flow_q2 (m³/h)\n- permanent_flow_q3 (m³/h)\n- overload_flow_q4 (m³/h)\n- ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless)\n\nThat's 32 values × 6 attributes = 192 value entries. That's heavy JSON but accurate. The instructions emphasize matrix detection as MOST IMPORTANT, so I'll do it.\n\nAlternatively, to reduce output size, I could rely on matrix_tables for the full detail and provide model_level for key attributes. The R137 example shows both matrix_tables AND model_level. I'll follow that pattern but maybe model_level with all attributes is fine. Let me be thorough and generate it.\n\nType-level characteristics (Table 1):\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic flow metering\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" → value string \"1 and 2\"? Or list. The schema value can be string. I'll do value \"1 and 2\" or maybe [1,2]? The structured value rules say value must be number | {min,max} | string. So string \"1 and 2\". Hmm, or accuracy_classes in recommendation covers it. I'll include as string.\n- environmental_class: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\" — value string \"M1 / O\" with note? I'll keep \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\" as string.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: -25 to +55 °C → {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C)\" → value string \"T50\" with range? Could split: water_temperature_class: \"T50\", water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C. The original says T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C). T50 class means water temp up to 50°C, min +0.1°C. I'll do water_temperature_class: \"T50\" and water_temperature_range {min:0.1, max:50} °C. Good.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure_map: 1.6 MPa → value 1.6, unit MPa; also 16 bar parenthetical. I'll do value 1.6 unit \"MPa\". Could add secondary. Keep simple: value 1.6, unit_symbol \"MPa\".\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\" → string.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\" → string.\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → string or boolean true. I'll use value true? Safer: string \"intended to measure reverse flow\" or value: true. Hmm. I'll use value: \"reverse flow measurement supported\" — no, don't fabricate wording; just keep original text: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\".\n- pressure_loss_class: two variants — full bore: Δp 10 (0.10 bar); reduced bore: Δp 40 (0.40 bar). This is config-dependent → config_level with axis \"bore_variant\". values: [{condition: \"full bore\", value: 10}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: 40}] with attribute pressure_loss_class, unit null? The class is Δp 10 meaning pressure loss coefficient; actual value 0.10 bar. I could do attribute pressure_loss with unit bar: {condition: \"full bore\", value: 0.1}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: 0.4}. And pressure_loss_class: full bore \"Δp 10\", reduced \"Δp 40\". I'll include both maybe. Keep config_level entries:\n  - attribute: pressure_loss_class, axis: bore_variant, values: [{condition: \"full bore\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}]. Or numeric with bar. I'll do numeric: attribute pressure_loss_max, unit bar: 0.1 / 0.4, and class strings separately. Let me do:\n    - {attribute: \"pressure_loss_class\", axis: \"bore_variant\", values: [{condition: \"full bore\", value: \"Δp 10\"}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: \"Δp 40\"}]}\n    - {attribute: \"maximum_pressure_loss\", axis: \"bore_variant\", unit_symbol: \"bar\", values: [{condition: \"full bore\", value: 0.1}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: 0.4}]}\n- power_supply: \"Replaceable battery (2.9 – 3.7 V)\" → power_supply_type: \"Replaceable battery\", battery_voltage_range: {min: 2.9, max: 3.7} V.\n- software_identification: \"See table 5\" — maybe skip or note.\n\nFootnote: The reduced bore asterisk: \"* For the reduced bore (RB) version, the size mentioned is the process connection size. On the inscriptions of the water meter the reduced bore meter will be identified as 'DNxxx RB'.\" → footnotes[] with marker \"*\". And Table 4 footnote: \"* This display indication range is only available with software revision 2.0.52 or later, other software versions shall not be used for meter size DN600. Additionally, this display indication range is only available with standard cover, IP68 cover shall not be used for meter size DN600.\"\n\nFootnote markers: use \"*\" for both? Two different footnotes with same marker \"*\" but different tables. Schema footnotes[]: marker, text. I can have two entries with marker \"* (Table 3)\" and \"* (Table 4)\"? Better: marker \"*\" and text prefixed with context. Hmm. I'll use markers \"*1\" no... Let me just use marker \"*\" with text including which table: The footnote text itself is distinct; I could add \"(Table 3)\" context in text. Actually the first footnote appears under Table 3 (class 2 table, reduced bore rows). The second under Table 4. I'll list:\n- {marker: \"*\", text: \"For the reduced bore (RB) version, the size mentioned is the process connection size. On the inscriptions of the water meter the reduced bore meter will be identified as \\u201cDNxxx RB\\u201d. (Applies to Table 3)\"}\n- {marker: \"*\", text: \"This display indication range is only available with software revision 2.0.52 or later, other software versions shall not be used for meter size DN600. Additionally, this display indication range is only available with standard cover, IP68 cover shall not be used for meter size DN600. (Applies to Table 4)\"}\n\nHmm, adding \"(Applies to Table X)\" is slight fabrication but clarifying context; acceptable? The rule \"Don't fabricate\" refers to field values. Adding clarifying context might be risky. Alternative: use markers \"*\" and \"**\"? But the original uses \"*\" for both. I could mark footnote_markers on the DN600 indicating range value as [\"*\"] and on reduced bore rows... The matrix tables preserve the raw data anyway. For footnotes I'll include both with marker \"*\" and distinguish by table in a separate way — maybe marker \"* (Table 3)\" and \"* (Table 4)\". I think that's reasonable and transparent. Actually simpler: keep marker \"*\" for both entries; the text differs. JSON allows duplicate markers. Fine.\n\nTable 4 indicating device: meter size groups:\n- DN50: indicating range 99999 (99.999 using dots as thousands separator — European format! \"99.999\" = 99999), verification scale interval 0.0001 m³\n- DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125: 999999, 0.001\n- DN150: 999999, 0.01\n- DN200 till DN350: 9999999, 0.01\n- DN400 till DN500: 9999999, 0.1\n- DN600: 99999999*, 0.1\n\nYes, \"99.999\" in European convention with dots as thousand separators. So indicating range DN50 = 99,999 m³. And \"9.999.999\" = 9,999,999. DN600 \"99.999.999\" = 99,999,999.\n\nNote the class-1/2 flow tables used commas as decimals (0,315 = 0.315). Table 4 uses dots as thousands separators. Converting: 99.999 → 99999; 999.999 → 999999; 9.999.999 → 9999999; 99.999.999 → 99999999.\n\nMatrix table for Table 4: rows with meter_sizes (string like \"DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125\" or list), indicating_range_m3, verification_scale_interval_m3, footnote_markers.\n\nTable 5 software versions: two tables (page 5 and page 6). Combine into one matrix_table \"Approved software versions\" with rows:\n- 9.5.28: OTP CRC bdFc, APP CRC 63b5\n- 2.0.30: 0571, 1644\n- 2.0.33: 0571, 7e5d\n- 2.0.34: 0571, 6D40\n- 2.0.35: 0571, E151\n- 2.0.37: 0571, 019A\n- 2.0.38: 0571, 6902\n- 2.0.40: 0571, 7214\n- 2.0.42: 0571, B459\n- 2.0.43: 0571, 2191\n- 2.0.44: 0571, 5ab6\n- 2.0.46: 0571, E865\n- 2.0.50: 0571, 3cc5\n- 2.0.51: 0571, 6b5c\n- 2.0.52: 0571, 71ca\n- 2.0.53: 0571, 05A2\n- 2.0.54: 0571, 1AD3\n- 2.0.55: 0571, 48BE\n- 2.0.56: 0571, BFF6\n- 2.0.57: 0571, 4C4E\n- 2.0.58: 0571, 846C\n- 2.0.59: 0571, 078E\n- 2.0.60: 0571, 1291\n- 2.0.61: 0571, ECD7\n\nColumns: software_version, crc_otp, crc_app, remarks.\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-13200198-01 Revision 1 dated 7 July 2022, 29 pages, role type_evaluation_report (it's likely the Type Evaluation Report). The cert says \"tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". The first is likely the type evaluation report. Roles: I'll assign \"type_evaluation_report\" to NMi-13200198-01 and \"test_report\" to others. Hmm, without explicit roles, this is a judgment. The doc mentions \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\" generally. First report (2022, 29 pages) is plausibly the TER; later ones are additions/modifications. I'll mark the first as type_evaluation_report and the rest as test_report.\n\n- No. NMi-3531004-01 revision 1 dated 4 July 2023, 20 pages\n- No. NMi-3891220-01 dated 17 April 2025, 19 pages\n- No. NMi-3891220-02 dated 31 July 2025, 18 pages\n\nDocumentation folder no. T10554-4 → test_reports entry with role documentation_file? The schema includes role documentation_file. \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded in the Documentation folder no. T10554-4.\" Yes, include: {id: \"T10554-4\", role: \"documentation_file\"}.\n\nRevision history:\n- Initial, 11 January 2021, \"-\"\n- 1, 17 March 2022, Added production location.\n- 2, 15 June 2023, Extension of line sizes and additional software revisions\n- 3, 4 July 2023, Correction of small error in Q4 flowrate for DN600\n- 4, 17 May 2024, Correction of small error in Q4 flowrate for DN200/DN250\n- 5, 17 April 2025, Addition of the reduced bore version with ratio 400 and software version 2.0.60\n- 6, 31 July 2025, Addition of horizontal housing, IP68 variant and software version 2.0.61\n\nRevision as string: \"initial\", \"1\", ..., \"6\".\n\nProduction location: Badger Meter Czech Republic s.r.o., Maňkova 2082/26, 621 00 Brno, Czech Republic. Where to put? Schema doesn't have production locations. Could add to manufacturers[] as additional manufacturer entry? The schema manufacturers[] has name, address_lines. The production location is a manufacturer site. Adding it as a manufacturer seems reasonable — it's where the water meter is produced. I'll add it as a second manufacturers entry. That's a reasonable interpretation, not fabrication.\n\nCertificate fields:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\" (revision separate). The schema: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. Revision history captures revisions; certificate number maybe include \" revision 6\"? I'd put number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\" and maybe note revision 6. There's no revision field at cert level; revision_history covers it. But to be safe, number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03 revision 6\"? Hmm. I'll use \"R49/2013-A-NL1-21.03\" as number. Since revision 6 is the current revision per revision_history (revision \"6\", date 31 July 2025). date_issued: \"2025-07-31\".\n\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"3891220\"\n- page_total: 7\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nRecommendation:\n- id: \"R49-1\" (or \"R49\"? The cert cites R 49-1 (2013). Canonical: \"R49-1\". I'll go with \"R49-1\".) Hmm, the schema example: 'id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\")'. R49-1 is a sub-part; keeping \"R49-1\" is more precise. I'll use \"R49-1\".\n- edition: 2013 — wait, edition (int). \"R 49-1 (2013)\" — 2013 is the edition year. int 2013.\n- amendment: null/omit.\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [1, 2]\n\nComponents: Does the cert name sub-assemblies? It mentions \"indicating device\" (Table 4) and sensor implied. The water meter consists of sensor + calculator + indicating device typically, but the cert doesn't explicitly name components beyond \"indicating device\". I could add a component: role \"indicating device\". Hmm, \"include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies\". Table 4 is \"General characteristics of the indicating device\". I'll add components: [{role: \"indicating device\", type_designations: []}]. Eh, maybe characteristics instead. I'll include a single component entry for the indicating device since it's explicitly named. Actually I think it's fine either way; I'll include it as a component with characteristics indicating range info? The Table 4 data is per meter size group — that's matrix/table territory. I already have it in matrix_tables. I'll include the component minimal: role \"indicating device\", type_designations: [\"M5000\"]? No — don't fabricate. The indicating device is part of M5000. I'll just do {role: \"indicating device\"}. Hmm, is that useful? It's named in the cert, so permitted. I'll include it with no type_designations (omit).\n\nActually, maybe better to skip components since it's just a part of the same instrument, and Table 4 data lives in matrix_tables + could add indicating range to model_level? The indicating range varies by size group — I could add model_level attributes indicating_range (m³) and verification_scale_interval (m³) per model. That's more consistent with the schema. Let me add model_level entries:\n- indicating_range: unit m³, per model values (based on size group; DN600 has footnote \"*\")\n- verification_scale_interval: unit m³, per model.\n\nThat adds two more attributes × 32 models. The JSON is getting large but it's what the schema wants. Alternatively keep Table 4 only in matrix_tables (which the R137 example does for flow table but ALSO populates model_level for the key flow attributes). I'll do: matrix_tables for all four tables, plus model_level for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, diameter, indicating_range, verification_scale_interval. Hmm, that's a lot. Let me consider size: 32 models × 8 attributes ≈ 256 value objects. Each value object ~ {\"model\": \"DN50 class 2 reduced bore\", \"value\": 0.158}. It's fine — better complete.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the model naming. Maybe shorter: model_id \"DN50-C1\", \"DN50-C2\", \"DN50-C2-RB\"? The cert identifies reduced bore as \"DNxxx RB\". So model ids:\n- Class 1: DN50, DN65, ..., DN600 → but these conflict with class 2 same size. Need class in the id.\n\nMaybe model_id: \"M5000 DN50 class 1\" is verbose. I'll use \"DN50 class 1\" style, and \"DN50 RB class 2\" for reduced bore. Reduced bore only exists for class 2. Cert says reduced bore identified as \"DNxxx RB\". So:\n- \"DN50 class 1\"\n- \"DN65 class 1\"\n- ... 14 entries\n- \"DN50 class 2\", \"DN50 RB class 2\", \"DN65 class 2\", \"DN65 RB class 2\", \"DN80 class 2\", \"DN80 RB class 2\", \"DN100 class 2\", \"DN100 RB class 2\", \"DN125 class 2\", \"DN150 class 2\", \"DN200 class 2\", \"DN250 class 2\", \"DN300 class 2\", \"DN350 class 2\", \"DN400 class 2\", \"DN450 class 2\", \"DN500 class 2\", \"DN600 class 2\" — 18 entries.\n\nLabels: model_id suffices; maybe add label like \"Class 1, DN50\" — optional. I'll add label with attributes: accuracy_class, bore_variant, nominal_diameter? Let me add attributes to each model: {\"accuracy_class\": 1, \"bore\": \"full\"} etc. That's helpful.\n\nActually, to keep the JSON manageable, I'll include attributes {accuracy_class, bore_variant} per model.\n\nFamily name: The cert says \"family of instruments\" and type M5000. family_name: \"M5000\" — the type designation serves as family name. I'll set family_name: \"M5000\".\n\nNow the matrix_tables. Schema: name, columns[], rows[] (object per row), description, footnotes[]. Also the R137 example includes column_units. I'll include column_units.\n\nTable 2: name \"General characteristics of the family of instruments with accuracy class 1\"\ncolumns: [\"meter_size\", \"diameter_in_outlet\", \"q1_minimum\", \"q2_transitional\", \"q3_permanent\", \"q4_overload\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, \"mm\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]\nrows: 14 rows.\n\nValues (converting commas):\nDN50: 50, 0.315, 0.504, 63, 78.75, 200\nDN65: 65, 0.5, 0.8, 100, 125, 200\nDN80: 80, 0.8, 1.28, 160, 200, 200\nDN100: 100, 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\nDN125: 125, 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250\nDN150: 150, 3.9375, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160\nDN200: 200, 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\nDN250: 250, 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\nDN300: 300, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\nDN350: 350, 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\nDN400: 400, 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\nDN450: 450, 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\nDN500: 500, 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\nDN600: 600, 50.4, 80.64, 6300, 7875, 125\n\nTable 3: class 2, columns same plus bore_variant note in meter_size string:\nrows:\nDN50: 50, 0.315, 0.504, 63, 78.75, 200\nDN50 reduced bore: 50, 0.158, 0.25, 63, 78.75, 400\nDN65: 65, 0.5, 0.8, 100, 125, 200\nDN65 reduced bore: 65, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\nDN80: 80, 0.8, 1.28, 160, 200, 200\nDN80 reduced bore: 80, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\nDN100: 100, 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\nDN100 reduced bore: 100, 0.63, 1.00, 250, 312.5, 400\nDN125: 125, 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250\nDN150: 150, 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\nDN200: 200, 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\nDN250: 250, 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\nDN300: 300, 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\nDN350: 350, 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\nDN400: 400, 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\nDN450: 450, 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\nDN500: 500, 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\nDN600: 600, 40, 64, 10000, 12500, 250\n\nFor reduced bore rows, add bore_variant column? I'll add a \"bore_variant\" column: \"full bore\"/\"reduced bore\" and meter_size clean \"DN50\". Plus footnote_markers for reduced bore rows: [\"*\"]. Columns: meter_size, bore_variant, diameter_in_outlet, q1_minimum, q2_transitional, q3_permanent, q4_overload, ratio_q3_q1. For table 2 bore_variant all \"full bore\". I could also add the \"*\" footnote to reduced bore rows via row key \"footnote_markers\": [\"*\"].\n\nTable 4: name \"General characteristics of the indicating device\"\ncolumns: meter_sizes, indicating_range, verification_scale_interval\ncolumn_units: [null, \"m³\", \"m³\"]\nrows:\n- {meter_sizes: \"DN50\", indicating_range: 99999, verification_scale_interval: 0.0001}\n- {meter_sizes: \"DN65; DN80; DN100; DN125\", indicating_range: 999999, verification_scale_interval: 0.001}\n- {meter_sizes: \"DN150\", indicating_range: 999999, verification_scale_interval: 0.01}\n- {meter_sizes: \"DN200 till DN350\", indicating_range: 9999999, verification_scale_interval: 0.01}\n- {meter_sizes: \"DN400 till DN500\", indicating_range: 9999999, verification_scale_interval: 0.1}\n- {meter_sizes: \"DN600\", indicating_range: 99999999, verification_scale_interval: 0.1, footnote_markers: [\"*\"]}\n\nfootnotes for this table: the software 2.0.52 note.\n\nTable 5: name \"Approved software versions\"\ncolumns: software_version, crc_otp, crc_app, remarks\nrows: 24 entries as listed above. CRC values strings: \"bdFc\", \"63b5\" etc.\n\nNote 2.0.60: \"1291(APP CRC)\" → 1291.\n\nNow type_level characteristics from Table 1:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic flow metering\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" — hmm maybe skip since recommendation.accuracy_classes covers. But it's in Table 1; include as value string \"1 and 2\"? Or [1,2]? Value must be number|minmax|string. String \"1 and 2\". Fine.\n- environmental_class: \"M1 / O (installed outdoors)\" — value string \"M1/O\" with note \"installed outdoors\"? I'll keep full string.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min: -25, max: 55}, \"°C\"\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, \"°C\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6, \"MPa\"\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" — as value string.\n- power_supply_type: \"Replaceable battery\"\n- battery_voltage_range: {min: 2.9, max: 3.7}, \"V\"\n\nFor value objects: shape {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}. The R137 example for model_level omits unit_id; values entries there are {\"model\": ..., \"value\": ...} only. For type_level, the strict rule says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. I'll include unit_id: null and omit footnote_markers when empty? The strict shape shows footnote_markers with value; I'll include footnote_markers only when non-empty to reduce noise... The example in rules shows footnote_markers: [\"1)\"] present. Rule 7 says record marker when referenced. I'll include footnote_markers only when non-empty; include unit_id: null always. Hmm, strictness: \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\". I'll include unit_id: null in all, and footnote_markers when applicable. For string values like \"E2\", unit_symbol null, unit_id null.\n\nConfig level:\n- pressure_loss: axis \"bore_variant\": values [{condition: \"full bore\", value: 0.1, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: 0.4, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}] — but config_level values shape per rule 5: \"{condition, value, unit_symbol}\". Also maybe footnote_markers. OK.\n- pressure_loss_class: [{condition: \"full bore\", value: \"Δp 10\"}, {condition: \"reduced bore\", value: \"Δp 40\"}]\n\nModel level: For each attribute, values across 32 models. Attributes:\n1. diameter_in_outlet (mm) — same across classes for a given size.\n2. q1_minimum (m³/h)\n3. q2_transitional (m³/h)\n4. q3_permanent (m³/h)\n5. q4_overload (m³/h)\n6. ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless)\n7. indicating_range (m³)\n8. verification_scale_interval (m³)\n\nFootnotes on model_level: reduced bore models reference \"*\" footnote for naming; DN600 indicating_range references \"*\" (Table 4 footnote). I'll add footnote_markers: [\"*\"] on reduced bore diameter/q1 etc.? That could get noisy. The footnote for RB is about naming/inscription, applies to the reduced bore variant generally. I'll add footnote_markers [\"*\"] to the reduced bore rows in the matrix table only, and to DN600 indicating_range values in model_level. Keep model_level clean otherwise.\n\nLet me now decide model ids and enumerate. I'll use these model_ids:\nClass 1 (14): \"DN50 class 1\", \"DN65 class 1\", \"DN80 class 1\", \"DN100 class 1\", \"DN125 class 1\", \"DN150 class 1\", \"DN200 class 1\", \"DN250 class 1\", \"DN300 class 1\", \"DN350 class 1\", \"DN400 class 1\", \"DN450 class 1\", \"DN500 class 1\", \"DN600 class 1\"\nClass 2 (18): \"DN50 class 2\", \"DN50 RB class 2\", \"DN65 class 2\", \"DN65 RB class 2\", \"DN80 class 2\", \"DN80 RB class 2\", \"DN100 class 2\", \"DN100 RB class 2\", \"DN125 class 2\", \"DN150 class 2\", \"DN200 class 2\", \"DN250 class 2\", \"DN300 class 2\", \"DN350 class 2\", \"DN400 class 2\", \"DN450 class 2\", \"DN500 class 2\", \"DN600 class 2\"\n\nmodel_family.models entries with attributes:\n{\"model_id\": \"DN50 class 1\", \"label\": \"M5000 DN50, accuracy class 1\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_class\": 1, \"bore_variant\": \"full bore\", \"nominal_size\": \"DN50\"}}\n\nHmm labels maybe redundant. I'll include label simpler: \"DN50, class 1\". Or omit label. The schema: model_id, label, attributes{}. label optional-ish (\"model_id, label, attributes{}\"). I'll include label.\n\nGiven output length constraints, this will be a long JSON but acceptable.\n\nNow model_level arrays. Let me tabulate all data:\n\nSizes with diameter: DN50:50, DN65:65, DN80:80, DN100:100, DN125:125, DN150:150, DN200:200, DN250:250, DN300:300, DN350:350, DN400:400, DN450:450, DN500:500, DN600:600.\n\ndiameter_in_outlet values per model (32): straightforward — RB models same diameter as normal (process connection size).\n\nq1_minimum (m³/h):\nC1: DN50 0.315, DN65 0.5, DN80 0.8, DN100 1, DN125 1.6, DN150 3.9375, DN200 6.25, DN250 10, DN300 15.625, DN350 15.625, DN400 25, DN450 39.375, DN500 39.375, DN600 50.4\nC2: DN50 0.315, DN50RB 0.158, DN65 0.5, DN65RB 0.25, DN80 0.8, DN80RB 0.4, DN100 1, DN100RB 0.63, DN125 1.6, DN150 2.52, DN200 6.4, DN250 6.4, DN300 10, DN350 10, DN400 16, DN450 25.2, DN500 25.2, DN600 40\n\nq2_transitional:\nC1: 0.504, 0.8, 1.28, 1.6, 2.56, 6.3, 10, 16, 25, 25, 40, 63, 63, 80.64\nC2: DN50 0.504, RB 0.25, DN65 0.8, RB 0.4, DN80 1.28, RB 0.64, DN100 1.6, RB 1.00, DN125 2.56, DN150 4.032, DN200 10.24, DN250 10.24, DN300 16, DN350 16, DN400 25.6, DN450 40.32, DN500 40.32, DN600 64\n\nq3_permanent:\nC1: 63, 100, 160, 250, 400, 630, 1000, 1600, 2500, 2500, 4000, 6300, 6300, 6300\nC2: DN50 63 (RB 63), DN65 100 (RB 100), DN80 160 (RB 160), DN100 250 (RB 250), DN125 400, DN150 630, DN200 1600, DN250 1600, DN300 2500, DN350 2500, DN400 4000, DN450 6300, DN500 6300, DN600 10000\n\nq4_overload:\nC1: 78.75, 125, 200, 312.5, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000, 3125, 3125, 5000, 7875, 7875, 7875\nC2: DN50 78.75 (RB 78.75), DN65 125 (RB 125), DN80 200 (RB 200), DN100 312.5 (RB 312.5), DN125 500, DN150 787.5, DN200 2000, DN250 2000, DN300 3125, DN350 3125, DN400 5000, DN450 7875, DN500 7875, DN600 12500\n\nratio_q3_q1:\nC1: 200, 200, 200, 250, 250, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 125\nC2: DN50 200 (RB 400), DN65 200 (RB 400), DN80 200 (RB 400), DN100 250 (RB 400), DN125 250, DN150 250, DN200 250, DN250 250, DN300 250, DN350 250, DN400 250, DN450 250, DN500 250, DN600 250\n\nindicating_range (m³): by